iting isn't true or  whatever point is the 24/7 and global  communications we have has altered the  actual dynamic of reporting and the  truth of the matter is I think the media  is struggles with that because a lot of  this war when you're in Baghdad you were  really writing for an audience in  Washington  unlike even the Vietnam War where the  battle reports would have just been  fairly straightforward at least in the  you know early to middle days of the war  so I don't know if that answers your  question but you know I think and one of  the premises of book is that friction is  inevitable and and the government has to  struggle with how it how much  information it releases and I don't know  sort of a historical side note I'll  mention my next book is gonna be a  smaller book about a documentary  filmmaker marine named Norman hatch who  was the only marine on Tarawa to film  the bloody battle there and he shared  with me recently what happened when his  footage went back to Washington and  Franklin Roosevelt saw it Roosevelt  didn't think at the time that they  should wasn't sure that they should  release the battle footage so he untold  that that Roosevelt conferred with the  time-life correspondent whose name was  Robert Sherrod very famous war  correspondent Bob sherrod my father  happened to know him  and Bob Sharat called him down from  Washington asking the question it said  absolutely mr. president we need to  release this information because at the  time America didn't realize what a long  long war we were going to be in with  Japan so I say that because that was 70  years ago and these questions about  access and you know do we release  certain things and not others have  always been there the difference is the  instantaneous nature that we showed the  picture of Abu Ghraib up there cell  phones  I mean I'm told right now that inside  the Pentagon there's a debate over  whether cellphones have to be banned for  combat troops why because there's been  cas